The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its own table, built from the
export at render time, has said otherwise since the day it was written: two causes, and a footnote
about reports read as a one-day move. This page reads the same data, counts as the desk does, and gives each problem a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">

Three problems were merged into #4471 because their subject lines matched
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). One queued fix covers one of them.

</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**`parser` · month and day flipped by the shared parser.** Every tenant on file writes the day
first. The shared parser reads month first, so a booking typed as 7 April goes out as 4 July. It hits
every channel of every tenant. This is the bug the queued fix is for.

**`hint` · month and day flipped by the booking-v3 form.** The booking-v3 form prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint says, month first, and the form reads it
the tenant's way, day first. Same symptom, other direction, different code: fixing the parser does not
touch it. Only booking-v3 tenants, only through the form.

**`one-day-move` · the date moves by one day.** No flip. One meeting shows Tuesday in Lisbon and
Monday in Toronto; 6 October shows as a Monday; a whole month is one day off. The desk's notes mark
these five reports as a one-day move. The desk counts them where it files them, under the tenant's
cause, and footnotes them there; this page keeps that count and gives them a row and a ticket of their
own. A day boundary is being crossed somewhere between the booking's time zone and the invite's.

## What engineering did

#4471 was closed on 5 October. Per problem:

| problem | outcome | engineering's note |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | fixed | `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` |
| `hint` | won't fix | booking-v3 form retired |
| `one-day-move` | not addressed | the five reports went out with the parser fix, counted under it by tenant; nothing in the fix touches a day boundary |

So the 379 parser reports have their fix, the 33 hint reports lose their cause with the form, and the
five one-day moves are closed without a change. If they come back, they come back as a new ticket.

## How to tell them apart

1. Is the date flipped (day and month swapped) or moved by one day? Moved: `one-day-move`.
2. Flipped and the report came through the booking form of a booking-v3 tenant: `hint`.
3. Flipped, anything else: `parser`.

Intake's tag cannot do this. It guesses from the reporter's browser language, and the browser
language says nothing about the tenant's booking form or channel. The table above counts how often
the guess was right.

## What changes on the desk

- #4471 is closed. Watch for new one-day-move reports: nothing shipped for them, and matching on
  subject will merge them into the next flipped-date ticket again.
- Subject lines did not tell these apart, and bodies did. Reading one body per tenant and channel
  would have split the ticket in April.

